The `useState` hook lets you add local state to functional components.

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { View, Button, Text } from 'react-native';

export default function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>Count: {count}</Text>
      <Button title="Increment" onPress={() => setCount(count + 1)} />
    </View>
  );
}
```

You can manage multiple pieces of state by calling `useState` multiple times.

```jsx
const [name, setName] = useState('');
const [age, setAge] = useState(0);
```

Control when the effect runs by adding dependencies.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Count changed:", count);
}, [count]);
```

Run logic only on component mount or unmount using an empty dependency array or return a cleanup function.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Component mounted");
  return () => console.log("Component unmounted");
}, []);
```
